  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nToday - Wednesday: Cool and Mostly Dry\n\nA parting upper tropospheric shortwave trough exits the region \nby this afternoon and could lead to scattered flurries north of \nI-94 through midday, but the main forecast message for the start\nof the week is for dry conditions as surface high pressure \nmoves through. As the center of this high arrives tonight into \nTuesday morning, some of the coldest temperatures of the season \nare progged to occur. The current forecast has lows in the upper\nsingle digits to mid-teens, but there is a 10% chance that lows\ncould tank into the single digits areawide depending on how the\nwinds and high clouds shape up. The surface high anchors over \nthe Great Lakes for Tuesday into Wednesday, keeping a northeast \nfetch and seasonal temperatures in place through the week.\n\n\nThanksgiving: Potential Snow in Southern WI and Northeast IA\n\nThe forecast turns more interesting for midweek as a pair of\nnorthern and southern stream waves undergo a complex interaction\nthat could result in potentially significant snowfall somewhere\nbetween southern Wisconsin/northeast Iowa into Illinois. The\nscenario involves a pair of mid to upper level PV lobes that\neject with a meridional longwave trough over the Dakotas and \nTX/OK on Tuesday. A strengthening upstream ridge forces the \nnorthern stream vort lobe to sink southward and form a quasi- \nbarbell low structure with its southern counterpart across the \nmid to lower Mississippi River valley. The warm sector and \nattendant moisture from the southern system could get caught up\ninto the flow around the northern stream low, but with the \nsouthern low wrapping around the upstream flank of the mean \nlongwave trough and acting to block its forward momentum, the \neffective warm front stalls along a north to south axis for \npossibly up to 24 hours and drive a prolonged period of snow.\n\nThe end result is a potential boom or bust snow event for \nportions of the Midwest. There is an increasing number of \nensemble members that are depicting this scenario unfolding--\nmainly over Illinois--but given the complexities in the \ninteractions between these two systems, we remain too close to \nbe out of the woods. A look at individual ensemble member \nhistograms shows that, if this scenario unfolds, snow amounts \n(again, focused in Illinois) could exceed 6 inches (10% chance\nat any given location in central IL) with some rather \noutlandish outliers that push into the 10-12+ inch range. \nSouthern Wisconsin is most at risk for being impacted by the \nnorthern side of this system (current probabilities for 1-3\" of \nsnow are around 10-30%), but to what degree remains to be seen \nand there will likely be a sharp cutoff in snow amounts on the \nedges of the snow band. Given the high traffic volume expected \nduring this holiday period, close attention will need to be paid\nas the forecast unfolds over the coming days.",
